-- What a normalized call will not accept, at the call site and at the declaration.
--
-- Two halves. The call-site half is everything `mapArguments` refuses - a name no parameter has, a
-- parameter filled twice, a positional argument after a named one, a position left out with no
-- default - and the point of each is that it is *one* diagnostic naming the thing that is wrong,
-- rather than the arity message a call whose count is perfectly fine would get.
--
-- The declaration half is the three positions a default cannot be written on, and each of those is a
-- rule about the caller rather than about the constant: a `return` parameter has no caller storage
-- when it is left out, a `&` one is written through, and one declared as a type variable has no type
-- for the constant to be of.

fn open(path: Int, mode: Int = 1, buffered: Int = 2) -> Int = path + mode + buffered

-- A name nothing declares. The count is right, so the arity message would be true and useless.
fn unknownName() -> Int = open(1, format: 2)

-- The same parameter reached twice - once by position and once by name.
fn twice() -> Int = open(1, 2, mode: 3)

-- A positional argument after a named one. Which parameter it fills would depend on which ones the
-- names before it took, so the rule is that it may not be written rather than that it is guessed.
fn positionalAfterNamed() -> Int = open(mode: 2, 5)

fn required(a: Int, b: Int, c: Int = 3) -> Int = a + b + c

-- A position left out that has no default, named by the parameter it is - because a call that used
-- names at all is not one whose problem is a count.
fn missingNamed() -> Int = required(b: 2, c: 4)

-- The same mistake in a call that wrote no names, which *is* a count - and the count is a range,
-- since one of the three positions has a default.
fn missingPositional() -> Int = required(1)

-- More arguments than there are parameters, with the range said the same way.
fn tooMany() -> Int = required(1, 2, 3, 4)

-- A position the count reached and the order did not: one argument is a legal number of them, and
-- it filled the defaulted parameter because that is what a positional argument does. The count is
-- both true and satisfied here, so what the message hands over is the name that fixes it.
fn middleDefault(a: Int = 1, b: Int) -> Int = a * 10 + b
fn orderNotCount() -> Int = middleDefault(4)

-- A call through a function *value*, which knows its parameters' names and has no defaults to fill
-- a position with - so the same two mistakes are the same two messages, said about the type.
fn throughValueName() -> Int:
    let f = (from: Int, take: Int) -> from - take
    return f(from: 1, count: 2)

fn throughValueShort() -> Int:
    let f = (from: Int, take: Int) -> from - take
    return f(from: 1)

-- A default that is not a constant. The rule is the field default's, stated once for both.
fn computed(x: Int, y: Int = required(1, 2)) -> Int = x + y

-- A default whose constant is not a value of the parameter's type.
fn wrongType(x: Int, flag: Bool = 3) -> Int = x

-- The three positions that cannot carry one at all.
data Box {value: Int}

fn returnDefault(return self: Box = 1) -> Int = self.value
fn mutableDefault(&slot: Int = 1) -> Int = slot
fn (Num(a)) genericDefault(x: a = 1) -> Int = 0

{-
   A `@lazy` parameter *can*, and this is the case that says so - Design-Test.md §11.1's F3.

   It could not until the objection was read back: "the default would be one more expression the call
   site did not write and the callee may not run". There are no expressions here. Every default in
   this language is `evaluateConstant`'s answer, so what a call site that leaves the position out is
   given is a constant in a thunk - nothing to run, and nothing to observe if it is never forced.

   The default is read at the *promised* type rather than at the thunk's, which is the only thing the
   position changes: `2` is a constant `Int` and not a constant `{} -> Int`. The non-constant case is
   refused by the same rule as every other default, which is the first line of this file's expected
   errors and not a rule of its own.
-}
fn lazyDefault(x: Int, @lazy y: Int = 2) -> Int = x

-- A loop's iterator, where the count message is about the arguments written before the body - and
-- is therefore the wrong thing to say about a name. The two cases are what tells them apart.
iter fn steps(limit: Int, from: Int = 0) -> Int:
    let &i = from :: Int

    while i < limit:
        yield i
        i = i + 1

fn loopBadName() -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for i in steps(upto: 3):
        total = total + i

    return total

fn loopTooMany() -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for i in steps(1, 2, 3):
        total = total + i

    return total

class Scale(a):
    fn scale(it: a, by: Int = 2) -> Int

-- An instance may not declare a default: what a call site leaves out is read off the class signature
-- before the instance is selected, so one written here is a constant nothing ever passes.
instance Scale(Int):
    fn scale(it: Int, by: Int = 5) -> Int = it * by

-- A name on a class function. The class half is matched per candidate, so a name nothing declares
-- makes every candidate fail - and "no class function scale accepts (Int, Int)" would be true and
-- say nothing about the `times:` that is wrong.
fn classBadName() -> Int = scale(2, times: 3)

fn main() -> Int = open(1)
